-- `@inline(always)` on a body the resolver's one-expression graft cannot take - inventory D12.
--
-- Each of these was a hard error until 2026-09-06: a branch, a call, several `return`s, a generic
-- body that is all three. The attribute now means one thing, which is what
-- lets `lib/` write a container's `get` as a body rather than leaving it to a generator.
--
-- What the fixture asserts is the *pair* of goldens. `.mir.expect` is what the resolver
-- produced, and every call below is still in `main` there, because the graft declined all four.
-- `.ssa.opt.expect` is the same program after the optimizer, and none of them is: the inliner
-- admits an `@inline(always)` callee against its ceiling alone, weighing nothing else in its
-- table. See `compiler/resolve_ssa/opt/inline.cpp`, which argues which pass carries out which half.
--
-- The one shape that is still refused is a body that calls itself - Reject.Inline.yana.

-- A branch. The plain form of what the attribute could not carry before.
@inline(always)
fn absolute(x: Int) -> Int = if x < 0 then 0 - x else x

-- A call to something else, which is the clause that said what the old restriction was *for*: an
-- expansion is a run of computation and a call brings a frame with it. The inliner has a frame.
fn decrement(x: Int) -> Int = x - 1

@inline(always)
fn stepped(x: Int) -> Int = decrement(x) + 1

-- Several `return`s, so the graft would have had a join to build rather than a value to hand back.
@inline(always)
fn clamped(x: Int, limit: Int) -> Int:
    if x > limit: return limit
    if x < 0 - limit: return 0 - limit
    return x

-- The generic form, which is the case D10 is about. A generic `@inline(always)` callee is
-- specialized rather than erased - a body spent at every site has no code of its own to share - so
-- what the site declines to graft is the *specialization* below the generic one, and that is what
-- the inliner takes. Both are printed because this is the root module, whose functions are printed
-- whether or not anything still calls them.
@inline(always)
fn (Ord(a)) below(x: a, y: a) -> Bool = if x < y then True else False

-- Where the numbers come from, and it is `@noinline` on purpose: `main` over four literals folds to
-- one `ret` and asserts nothing about which pass removed which call. With one value the optimizer
-- cannot see, what stands in `main` is the four bodies themselves.
@noinline
fn source() -> Int = 0 - 4

fn main() -> Int:
    let a = absolute(source())
    let b = stepped(3)
    let c = clamped(99, 5)
    return if below(a, c) then a + b + c else 0
